UPHELD(high): I could not refute this; it is confirmed at origin/inkling2. In ios/AgentConsole/Inference/MLXBackend.swift, loadLarge sets MemoryBudget.pendingLoadKey at lines 138-139. It then runs `let loaded = try await VLMModelFactory.shared.loadContainer(...)` at lines 145-149, with no do/catch and no defer. The key is removed only at line 151, which runs only if the load succeeds. A git grep across ios/ finds no other code that removes pendingLoadKey: it appears only at MLXBackend.swift:139 and 151 and MemoryBudget.swift:35, 43 and 45. Any throw from loadContainer (a network or Hub error, a weight-shape mismatch, or a cancelled download task) leaves the key set. On the next launch, MemoryBudget.noteLaunch (MemoryBudget.swift:41-45, called from MLXBackend.swift:24) copies it to killedLoadKey. SettingsView.swift:119 then shows it as a load that iOS killed for using too much memory. The result is a false diagnosis, with no crash or data loss, so low severity is right. | fix: Clear the breadcrumb on every exit that leaves the process alive. Store a unique token with it, so a stale load cannot clear the breadcrumb of a newer load:

let token = UUID().uuidString
defaults.set(token, forKey: MemoryBudget.pendingLoadKey + ".id")
defaults.set("...", forKey: MemoryBudget.pendingLoadKey)
defer {
    if defaults.string(forKey: MemoryBudget.pendingLoadKey + ".id") == token {
        defaults.removeObject(forKey: MemoryBudget.pendingLoadKey)
        defaults.removeObject(forKey: MemoryBudget.pendingLoadKey + ".id")
    }
}

A simpler option is to wrap loadContainer in do/catch, remove the key in the catch, and rethrow. Only a process kill would then leave the key behind.
